abstract:
Forecast of weather and ocean conditions are carried out through the application of computational numerical models. However, the results of these models are only reliable when they are confronted with metocean data collected and available in real-time.
For regions of the South Atlantic, most real-time data collection are not available in real-time for international and specialized databases, like the Global Telecommunication System (GTS). In this way, the official weather forecast centre at sea for the South Atlantic, called Brazil Marine Meteorological Service (SMM), does not have access to this information and cannot use it to improve their forecast models and bulletins.

In order to help solve this problem, an open source platform called OCEANUM.LIVE (www.oceanum.live) was developed. Its objective is to represent an open-source, scalable, and low-maintenance real-time and unified metocean data system for the South Atlantic. The system is based on five steps:
- a data mining system to obtain data through different locations;
- a data processing system that converts data to international standards, and applies quality control codes to the data, using an open source library developed especially for this project;
- a geospatial relational database for data storage;
- an API to allow researchers and forecasting centres to access data;
- a web app for data visualization.

This platform is already used by SMM to support its forecasts. This poster will outline the tools used for this system, as well as the challenges to getting collaborators for it in Brazil.

With a plethora of available numerical models spanning many engineering disciplines nowadays, it is both an advantage and a challenge to select an appropriate model for a simulation task. The computational engines, or solvers, are often developed by researchers who work in their "niche" and therefore would be difficult to integrate with other solvers or modules. Conventional programs favour execution speed over I/O and thus inadvertently form a barrage to data interoperability.
Fortunately, contemporary simulation software engineering has adopted good practices such as using version control systems, CI/CD, extensive testing, and containerisation. In addition, modern programming features also allows rapid prototyping of physical processes and accelerates the contribution of the scientific community to software packages such as Firedrake and Oceananigans. In this talk, we present a case using the Firedrake-based Thetis hydrodynamic solver flexibly in various contexts:
Ramp-up runs from static (equilibrium) to fully-developed flow conditions;
Model calibration/validation;
Long simulations and hot-start;
Sensitivity analysis.
Through these cases we also implement containerisation, handling data input from multiple sources and output visualisation. The aim is for data reproducibility, objectiveness and reduction in the manual work involved during input preparation.
